# MLB-Quant

Time-aware quantitative research foundation for MLB starting-pitcher strikeout
and pitcher outs recorded markets. This repository is intentionally a foundation:
it contains architecture, configuration, contracts, and validation scaffolding,
but no predictive models, MLB data downloads, or sportsbook APIs.

## Scope

The project specializes exclusively in two pitcher-level targets:

- Starting-pitcher strikeouts
- Starting-pitcher outs recorded

Workload modeling is a supporting layer because expected pitch count, innings,
and opportunity are relevant to both targets.

## Architecture

```text
config/                         Versioned project configuration
data/
	raw/                          Immutable source extracts (not included)
	processed/                    Validated tables and DuckDB database
src/mlb_quant/
	data/                         Ingestion and source-data validation
	features/                     Leakage-controlled feature engineering
	models/                       Workload, strikeout, and outs models
	simulation/                   Distributions and Monte Carlo utilities
	backtesting/                  Expanding-window chronological evaluation
	evaluation/                   Metrics, calibration, and reliability analysis
	markets/                      Market normalization and EV analysis
	reports/                      Tables, figures, and research reports
models/artifacts/               Local fitted model artifacts (not included)
notebooks/                      Exploratory research notebooks
reports/                        Generated figures and tables
tests/                          Unit and regression tests
```

Each area has a deliberately small Python module so future implementations can
be added without making notebooks the production interface. DuckDB and Parquet
are intended to provide a local analytical storage layer; pandas and scikit-
learn can operate on extracted feature tables.

## Historical Statcast ingestion

The ingestion layer uses `pybaseball.statcast` and requests bounded date chunks
(weekly by default). A successful chunk is saved as an immutable Parquet file in
`data/raw/`, and `data/raw/statcast_manifest.json` records its date range, row
count, validation report, and UTC ingestion time. Re-running the same request
skips completed chunks; failed chunks can be retried without redownloading
successful chunks. Raw files are never transformed in place.

The CLI creates a processed starting-pitcher game table without rolling
statistics or predictive features:

```bash
mlb-quant-ingest \
	--start 2024-04-01 \
	--end 2024-04-07 \
	--chunk-days 7
```

The equivalent module invocation is:

```bash
python -m mlb_quant.data.cli --start 2024-04-01 --end 2024-04-07
```

Useful options are `--raw-dir`, `--processed-dir`, `--retries`, and
`--log-level`. The command prints the downloaded range, raw pitch-row count,
game count, starter-appearance count, validation problems, and sample processed
rows. The derived table is written to
`data/processed/starting_pitcher_games_<start>_<end>.parquet`.

The first real-world audit of the seven-day test window is recorded in
`reports/audits/statcast_2024-04-01_2024-04-07.md`. It compares a deterministic
20-appearance sample and all 88 game starter pairs against MLB StatsAPI and
documents the edge-case corrections before any larger download.

## Scaling 2021-2025

After the audit, the complete regular seasons can be downloaded in order with:

```bash
mlb-quant-scale
```

This uses the same weekly, resumable ingestion pipeline and stores raw chunks
under `data/raw/season=YYYY/`. Each season is validated before the next begins;
critical schema, identifier, date, duplicate, inning, side, or pitch-number
failures stop the run. Season tables are written to
`data/processed/starting_pitcher_games_YYYY.parquet`, and the final table is
`data/processed/starting_pitcher_games_2021_2025.parquet`. A JSON season report
is written alongside it. Games with anything other than two identified starters
are reported as unusual or incomplete rather than filled or discarded silently.

The scale command supports only 2021-2025. The 2026 season is intentionally
kept outside this pipeline for future out-of-sample and live testing.

Starter identification uses the first chronological Statcast pitch for each
game and inning side, rather than assuming a roster field is the starter.
Batters faced is the count of distinct at-bats attributed to that pitcher;
strikeouts, hits, walks, and outs are derived from terminal Statcast events.
The output retains source-row counts and the outs derivation method for audit.
All raw pitch-level fields are retained, including timestamps, pitch numbers,
events, descriptions, pitch types, handedness, and identifiers.

## Time-awareness and leakage policy

This system must only use information that was available before the game being
predicted. Every historical observation should carry an availability timestamp
or an unambiguous source date. Feature builders must receive a timezone-aware
prediction cutoff and include only records with:

```text
observation_time < prediction_cutoff
```

Do not use a game's final box score, post-game status, later roster update, or
any other information that became available after that cutoff. Backtests must
use chronological expanding or rolling training windows, with each test period
strictly after its training data. Any market prices used for an eventual wager
should also retain their quote timestamp and be evaluated at the exact decision
time.

The initial temporal guards in `data/ingestion.py` and `features/temporal.py`
reject naive datetimes. They are small enforcement points to build on as real
schemas and feature builders are added.

## Setup

Python 3.11 or newer is supported. In Codespaces, create an isolated
environment from the repository root:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python -m pip install --editable .
```

The core dependency set includes pandas, NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn, DuckDB,
PyArrow, matplotlib, requests, and joblib. Pytest and PyYAML are included for
validation and configuration loading.

## Validation

Run the focused foundation checks with:

```bash
python -m pytest
python -c "import duckdb, joblib, matplotlib, numpy, pandas, pyarrow, requests, scipy, sklearn; print('major dependency imports: ok')"
```

The ingestion CLI does download Statcast data when explicitly invoked; the
commands above do not make network requests.

## Suggested research flow

1. Ingest immutable source data and preserve source/availability timestamps.
2. Validate identities, game dates, pitcher starts, and target definitions.
3. Build pre-game features using an explicit prediction cutoff.
4. Fit workload, strikeout, and outs recorded components inside each training window.
5. Produce probability distributions, not only point estimates.
6. Run chronological backtests and evaluate calibration separately from ranking.
7. Compare model probabilities with timestamped sportsbook markets and calculate EV after vig and execution assumptions.
8. Generate reproducible reports with the data/configuration/model versions recorded.
